      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 12 and 20, we can use the Euclidean algorithm, which involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of 0. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD.\n\n1. **First Step:**\n   Divide 20 by 12 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   20 \\div 12 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 20 - 12 \\times 1 = 8\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   20 = 12 \\times 1 + 8\n   \\]\n\n2. **Second Step:**\n   Now, take 12 and 8, and divide 12 by 8.\n   \\[\n   12 \\div 8 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 12 - 8 \\times 1 = 4\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   12 = 8 \\times 1 + 4\n   \\]\n\n3. **Third Step:**\n   Next, take 8 and 4, and divide 8 by 4.\n   \\[\n   8 \\div 4 = 2 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 8 - 4 \\times 2 = 0\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   8 = 4 \\times 2 + 0\n   \\]\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the last non-zero remainder is 4. Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 12 and 20 is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
